{% extends "tutorial_base.html.j2" %}

{% block tutorial_body %}
        <!-- Intro Card -->
        <div class="doc-intro-card">
          <h2>How Your CNC Controller Governs Plasma Torch Speed Across Plate &amp; Tubing</h2>
          <p>
            On a flat-sheet plasma table, calculating feedrates is straightforward: your torch moves along <code class="mono">X</code> and <code class="mono">Y</code> at a fixed speed (say, <code class="mono">2540 mm/min</code> or <code class="mono">100 IPM</code>). Your CNC controller simply measures the physical distance traveled divided by time.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
            When you mount a rotary chuck to cut round pipe, square tubing, or structural channel, standard CNC rules break down. The machine coordinates linear axes (<code class="mono">X, Y, Z</code>) in millimeters or inches with a rotary chuck axis (<code class="mono">A</code>) in angular degrees. If a G-code line commands <code class="mono">G1 A90 F2540</code>, what does <code class="mono">F2540</code> mean? 2540 degrees per minute? 2540 millimeters per minute at the surface of a 1" tube? Or 2540 mm/min on a 6" pipe?
          </p>
          <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
            Different CNC plasma controllers solve this in completely different ways: standard <code class="mono">G94</code> Units-Per-Minute, <code class="mono">G93</code> Inverse-Time (used on 4-axis rotary cutting across almost all controllers, including <code class="mono">SimCNC 3.6+</code> beta), or 4D Euclidean trajectory interpolation (<code class="mono">SimCNC &lt; 3.6</code>). While several controllers (Mach3, Mach4, UCCNC, MASSO, Centroid Acorn) allow entering a manual rotation radius on round pipe to scale G94 feedrates, that fails the moment you cut rectangular HSS, angle iron, or complex saddles where the radius from the rotation center to the cutting surface varies continuously. This guide explains what happens to your feedrates when flipping between Metric (<code class="mono">G21</code>) and Imperial (<code class="mono">G20</code>), why rotary <code class="mono">G93</code> feedrates stay 100% identical between inches and millimeters, and how to verify your G-code so you never blow out a pipe wall or stall a torch.
          </p>
          <div class="doc-intro-grid">
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Kinematics Modes</span>
              <span class="value">G94 Distance/Min • G93 Inverse-Time • SimCNC &lt; 3.6 4D Vector (G93 in 3.6+)</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Unit System</span>
              <span class="value">G21 (mm / mm/min) &bull; G20 (in / IPM)</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Controller Dialects</span>
              <span class="value">All 13 Major CNC Plasma Posts</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Quick Summary Callout -->
        <div class="doc-callout tip" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
          <div class="doc-callout-title">💡 Shop Rule of Thumb: Distance-Based vs. Time-Based Feeds</div>
          <p>
            When switching your machine profile from Metric (<code class="mono">G21</code>) to Imperial (<code class="mono">G20</code>):
            <strong>Flat-sheet <code class="mono">G94</code> feedrates divide by 25.4</strong> (<code class="mono">F2540.0 &rarr; F100.0</code>) because <code class="mono">F</code> represents distance per minute.
            <strong>4-axis rotary <code class="mono">G93</code> feedrates stay identical</strong> (<code class="mono">F1000.0 &rarr; F1000.0</code>) because <code class="mono">F</code> represents inverse time (<code class="mono">1 / &Delta;t</code>). The 25.4 unit factor cancels out because both cut speed and move distance divide by 25.4.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 1
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-1">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 01</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Why Torch Surface Speed Controls Cut Quality (and Why Rotary Axes Complicate It)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Plasma cutting relies on a delicate balance of heat and velocity: an electric arc ionizes compressed air or oxygen at over 20,000&deg;C to melt steel, while a high-pressure gas jet blows molten dross out the bottom of the kerf. If your torch travel speed across the steel surface drifts by even 15–20%, cut quality degrades immediately:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Cutting Too Slow:</strong> When the torch lingers too long over one spot, excessive heat soaks into the base metal. The kerf widens, the top edge rounds over, and heavy "low-speed dross" welds itself to the bottom of the cut, requiring an angle grinder or chipping hammer to clean. On thin-wall pipe (&le; 1/8" or 3 mm), moving too slow blows molten holes through the wall and spatters slag onto the inside of the opposite pipe wall.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Cutting Too Fast:</strong> When the torch travels faster than the plasma arc can penetrate, the arc column trails backward behind the nozzle tip (arc lag). This produces severe cut-edge bevel angle (often 10&deg;–20&deg; off square), high-speed glassy dross, and incomplete penetration where bottom webs fail to sever.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <div class="doc-callout danger" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">⚠ The Rotary Kinematics Mismatch: Millimeters vs. Degrees</div>
              <p>
                Linear axes (<code class="mono">X, Y, Z</code>) measure physical distance: millimeters (<code class="mono">mm</code>) or inches (<code class="mono">in</code>). But a rotary chuck (<code class="mono">A</code>) measures angle: degrees (<code class="mono">&deg;</code>). The physical distance the steel moves under the torch nozzle depends directly on the tube's outer radius (<code class="mono">R = OD / 2</code>):
              </p>
              <div style="background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5); padding: 0.85rem 1.15rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); margin: 0.75rem 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--amber-400);">
                Surface Distance (&Delta;s) = R &times; &Delta;A_radians = (OD / 2) &times; (&Delta;A_deg &times; &pi; / 180&deg;)
              </div>
              <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                Consider a 90&deg; rotation: On a <strong>1" OD tube (25.4 mm)</strong>, 90&deg; of chuck rotation sweeps <strong>19.95 mm (0.785")</strong> of steel past the torch. On a <strong>4" OD tube (101.6 mm)</strong>, that exact same 90&deg; rotation sweeps <strong>79.80 mm (3.142")</strong>, four times further! If a controller runs a fixed chuck rotation speed, the torch crawls on small tubing and sprints dangerously fast on large pipe.
              </p>
            </div>

            <p style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
              When cutting rectangular HSS tubing or angled pipe saddles, the challenge is even greater: the distance from the tube's rotation center to the cutting surface changes continuously on every millimeter of motion. At the center of a flat face, the radius is small; at the corner fillet, the radius spikes outward. To maintain constant surface speed, the controller must adjust axis speeds continuously.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 2
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-2">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 02</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title"><code class="mono">G94</code> Units-Per-Minute: Flat Sheet &amp; 3-Axis <code class="mono">Y</code>-Wrapped Rotary</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Standard 2D CNC plasma tables, mills, and routers operate in <code class="mono">G94</code> Units-Per-Minute mode. In <code class="mono">G94</code>, the <code class="mono">F</code> word dictates linear cutting speed:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Metric Mode (<code class="mono">G21</code>):</strong> <code class="mono">F</code> means <strong>millimeters per minute (mm/min)</strong>.</li>
              <li><strong>Imperial Mode (<code class="mono">G20</code>):</strong> <code class="mono">F</code> means <strong>inches per minute (IPM)</strong>.</li>
              <li><strong>The Conversion:</strong> Because 1 inch is exactly 25.4 mm:
                <div style="background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5); padding: 0.65rem 1rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); margin: 0.5rem 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--cyan-400);">
                  IPM = (mm/min) &divide; 25.4 &emsp;&hArr;&emsp; mm/min = IPM &times; 25.4
                </div>
              </li>
            </ul>

            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
              Here are standard shop feedrate equivalents across common cutting gauges:
            </p>
            <div style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 0.5rem 0 1.25rem;">
              <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.88rem; text-align: left; background: rgba(15, 20, 28, 0.6); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: 8px;">
                <thead>
                  <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light); background: rgba(255,255,255,0.04);">
                    <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.95rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-muted);">Gauge / Thickness</th>
                    <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.95rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-muted);">Metric (G21) Speed</th>
                    <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.95rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-muted);">Imperial (G20) Speed</th>
                    <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.95rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-muted);">Conversion Math</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);">
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem;">16ga (0.060" / 1.5 mm) Mild Steel (45A)</td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--cyan-400);">F6350.0 mm/min</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--amber-400);">F250.0 IPM</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem; color: var(--text-muted);">6350 &divide; 25.4 = 250.0</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);">
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem;">11ga (0.120" / 3.0 mm) Mild Steel (45A)</td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--cyan-400);">F3810.0 mm/min</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--amber-400);">F150.0 IPM</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem; color: var(--text-muted);">3810 &divide; 25.4 = 150.0</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);">
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem;">3/16" (0.188" / 4.75 mm) Mild Steel (45A)</td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--cyan-400);">F2540.0 mm/min</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--amber-400);">F100.0 IPM</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem; color: var(--text-muted);">2540 &divide; 25.4 = 100.0</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem;">1/4" (0.250" / 6.35 mm) Mild Steel (45A)</td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--cyan-400);">F1524.0 mm/min</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem;"><code class="mono" style="color: var(--amber-400);">F60.0 IPM</code></td>
                    <td style="padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem; color: var(--text-muted);">1524 &divide; 25.4 = 60.0</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <!-- Side by Side G-code Comparison -->
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(310px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-top: 1rem;">
              <div class="doc-code-block">
                <div class="doc-code-bar">
                  <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot cyan"></span> G21 Metric Flat Plate (RS-274 / grblHAL)</span>
                  <span class="doc-code-lang">G-CODE</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code>G21 ; Millimeters
G90 ; Absolute positioning
G94 ; Units/min feedrate mode
G00 X50.000 Y50.000
G38.2 Z-20.0 F1000.0 ; Touch-off probe
G92 Z0
G00 Z3.800 ; Pierce height
M03 S1 ; Torch fire
G04 P0.6 ; Pierce delay
G01 Z1.500 F1500.0 ; Drop to cut height
G01 X150.000 F2540.0 ; Cut at 2540 mm/min
G01 Y150.000 F2540.0
M05 ; Torch off</code></pre>
              </div>

              <div class="doc-code-block">
                <div class="doc-code-bar">
                  <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot"></span> G20 Imperial Flat Plate (RS-274 / grblHAL)</span>
                  <span class="doc-code-lang">G-CODE</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code>G20 ; Inches (4-decimal)
G90 ; Absolute positioning
G94 ; Units/min feedrate mode
G00 X1.9685 Y1.9685
G38.2 Z-0.7874 F39.4 ; Touch-off probe (&divide;25.4)
G92 Z0
G00 Z0.1500 ; Pierce height
M03 S1 ; Torch fire
G04 P0.6 ; Pierce delay
G01 Z0.0600 F59.1 ; Drop to cut height (&divide;25.4)
G01 X5.9055 F100.0 ; Cut at 100 IPM (2540/25.4)
G01 Y5.9055 F100.0
M05 ; Torch off</code></pre>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout info" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">⚙ The 3-Axis Rotary Wrap &amp; Fixed-Calibration Chuck Scaling (Langmuir FireControl)</div>
              <p>
                How do 3-axis tables like the <strong>Langmuir CrossFire PRO / XR</strong> (running FireControl <code class="mono">v1.6+</code> to <code class="mono">v24.1.6</code>) cut round pipe in <code class="mono">G94</code>? FireControl has no native 4th rotary axis driver, so you unplug one linear axis motor cable and plug in the rotary attachment. RotaryCAM prints a dynamic <code class="mono">Y-WRAP</code> or <code class="mono">X-WRAP</code> header banner matching your setup. Because CrossFire PRO and XR use <strong>dual Y1/Y2 gantry motors</strong>, select <strong>Tube Along Y</strong> when swapping the single-motor <strong>X-axis</strong> onto your rotary (<code class="mono">X-Wrap</code>).
              </p>
              <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                RotaryCAM unrolls round pipe circumference into linear travel: <code class="mono">Y = &pi; &times; OD &times; (A / 360&deg;)</code> (or <code class="mono">X</code> in <code class="mono">X-Wrap</code>). Standard <code class="mono">G94</code> feedrates apply (<code class="mono">F2540.0 mm/min</code> in <code class="mono">G21</code> drops to <code class="mono">F100.0 IPM</code> in <code class="mono">G20</code>), and rotary chucks now have a dedicated calibration option:
              </p>
              <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                <li>
                  <strong>Surface-Drive Friction Rollers (No Recalibration Needed):</strong> When your pipe rests directly on driven wheels, <code class="mono">1 mm</code> (or <code class="mono">1"</code>) of roller travel rolls <code class="mono">1 mm</code> (or <code class="mono">1"</code>) of pipe surface on any tube OD.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Rotary Chucks with Fixed-Calibration <code class="mono">Travel / Rev (L_rev)</code>:</strong> On geared rotary chucks, one full revolution requires a fixed linear command travel (<code class="mono">L_rev</code>). Enter your chuck's <code class="mono">Travel / Rev (L_rev)</code> once in Machine Settings and RotaryCAM outputs exact cylindrical DRO <code class="mono">G94</code> feed scaling across any pipe diameter without changing GRBL steps/mm between jobs, while keeping the <code class="mono">(PS&lt;feed&gt;)</code> footer at your true unscaled surface speed.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>FireControl G-Code Syntax:</strong> FireControl uses <code class="mono">H1</code> / <code class="mono">H0</code> for THC enable/disable, a two-stage <code class="mono">G38.2</code> / <code class="mono">G38.4</code> IHS loop with separated <code class="mono">0.020"</code> (<code class="mono">0.508 mm</code>) backlash vs. thin-sheet springback (auto-zeroed on rigid pipe and <code class="mono">&ge; 3/16"</code> plate), a mandatory <code class="mono">0.55 s</code> minimum <code class="mono">G4 P</code> ignition dwell after <code class="mono">M3</code> on 3D ramps (holding <code class="mono">H0</code> until cut height), and <code class="mono">M3</code> / <code class="mono">M5</code>.
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
                <em>Note:</em> 3-axis linear unrolling <strong>only works on constant-diameter round pipe</strong>. Square/rectangular HSS tubing, angle iron, and C-channel require 4 independent axes (<code class="mono">X, Y, Z, A</code>).
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 3
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-3">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 03</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title"><code class="mono">G93</code> Inverse-Time Rotary (<code class="mono">1 / Minutes</code>): Why <code class="mono">G20</code> &amp; <code class="mono">G21</code> Have Identical <code class="mono">F</code> Values</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              When cutting 4-axis rotary tubes (round pipe saddles, square/rectangular tubing, angle iron, C-channel), almost all professional CNC plasma controllers (including <strong>LinuxCNC QTPlasmaC, Mach4, MASSO G3, Mach3, Centroid Acorn, grblHAL, UCCNC, FlashCut, EdingCNC, PlanetCNC, ShopSabre WinCNC, and SimCNC 3.6+</strong>, which added native G93 inverse-time mode in beta in September 2026) use <strong><code class="mono">G93</code> Inverse-Time Feedrate Mode</strong>.
            </p>

            <div class="doc-callout tip" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">💡 What About the Controller's Built-In "Rotation Radius" Setting?</div>
              <p>
                Several controllers (including ArtSoft Mach3, Mach4, CNCdrive UCCNC, MASSO G3, and Centroid Acorn) provide a DRO setting where you can manually enter a workpiece rotation radius or diameter so the controller scales <code class="mono">G94</code> feedrates across round pipe. While convenient for basic round pipe parting, relying on a manual controller radius setting creates major shop hazards:
              </p>
              <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
                <li><strong>Operator Error:</strong> If you forget to update the DRO setting when switching from a 2" tube to a 4" pipe, the torch burns at the wrong speed.</li>
                <li><strong>Fails on Rectangular HSS &amp; Structural Shapes:</strong> On square/rectangular tubing, angle iron, and C-channel, the distance from the chuck centerline to the torch nozzle changes continuously (from a small radius at face centers to a large radius at corner fillets). A static radius setting cannot maintain constant surface speed.</li>
                <li><strong>Multi-Axis Vectors:</strong> On saddle fishmouths and branch joints, linear X/Y feed vectors combine with rotary A feed vectors in constantly shifting proportions.</li>
              </ul>
              <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                CAM-generated <code class="mono">G93</code> Inverse-Time eliminates these headaches: RotaryCAM computes the exact physical 3D surface speed along the true CAD model for every single segment. You get perfect surface speed across any shape or tube size without touching a radius DRO.
              </p>
              <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>ArtSoft Mach3 (<code class="mono">R3.043.062</code>) Rotary Check:</strong> Under <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time cutting, keep <strong>Use Radius for Feedrate UNCHECKED</strong>, <strong>G04 Dwell in ms UNCHECKED</strong>, <strong>LookAhead = 200</strong>, <strong>CV Feedrate OFF</strong>, and run a licensed Mach3 install (Demo caps at 500 lines). If you ever run a <code class="mono">G94</code> round-pipe job using Mach3's radius DRO, set Rotation Radius to <code class="mono">+0.0001</code> (never <code class="mono">0</code>). RotaryCAM restores <code class="mono">G94</code> immediately after every <code class="mono">M5</code> and coaxial scribe stroke, and filters sub-<code class="mono">0.002 mm</code> (<code class="mono">0.0001 in</code>) linear noise in <code class="mono">G93</code>.
              </p>
            </div>

            <h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: #fff; margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;">What Does an Inverse-Time Feedrate (<code class="mono">G93 F</code>) Actually Mean?</h3>
            <p>
              In <code class="mono">G93</code>, the <code class="mono">F</code> word does <em>not</em> mean distance per minute. It specifies the <strong>inverse of the time in minutes</strong> required to complete the move:
            </p>
            <div style="background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5); padding: 0.85rem 1.15rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); margin: 0.75rem 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--amber-400);">
              F = 1 / &Delta;t_minutes = 60 / &Delta;t_seconds &emsp;&hArr;&emsp; &Delta;t_seconds = 60 / F
            </div>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>If a G-code line has <code class="mono">F1000.0</code>, the move completes in <code class="mono">60 / 1000 = 0.060 seconds</code>.</li>
              <li>If a G-code line has <code class="mono">F500.0</code>, the move completes in <code class="mono">60 / 500 = 0.120 seconds</code>.</li>
              <li>If a G-code line has <code class="mono">F2000.0</code>, the move completes in <code class="mono">60 / 2000 = 0.030 seconds</code>.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: #fff; margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;">The Step-by-Step Proof: Why <code class="mono">F_G20</code> Equals <code class="mono">F_G21</code></h3>
            <p>
              How does CAM software calculate <code class="mono">F</code> in <code class="mono">G93</code>? It divides your desired plasma surface cut speed by the true physical length of the segment along the tube surface:
            </p>
            <div style="background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5); padding: 0.75rem 1rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); margin: 0.5rem 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--cyan-400);">
              F = (Surface Cutting Speed) / (Surface Move Distance)
            </div>

            <p style="margin-top: 0.85rem;">
              Let's walk through an actual rotary motion segment on 2" (50.8 mm OD, radius <code class="mono">R = 25.4 mm</code>) tubing using an exact 3-4-5 surface triangle:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Target Cut Speed:</strong> <code class="mono">2540.0 mm/min</code> = <code class="mono">100.0 IPM</code> (3/16" Mild Steel at 45A).</li>
              <li><strong>Move Segment:</strong> Linear <code class="mono">&Delta;X = 1.524 mm</code> (<code class="mono">0.0600"</code>) combined with chuck rotation <code class="mono">&Delta;A = 4.584&deg;</code>.</li>
              <li><strong>Surface Arc Distance:</strong> On 2" OD pipe, <code class="mono">&Delta;s_arc = 25.4 mm &times; (4.5837&deg; &times; &pi; / 180&deg;) = 2.032 mm</code> (<code class="mono">0.0800"</code>).</li>
              <li><strong>True 2D Surface Hypotenuse:</strong> <code class="mono">&Delta;s = &radic;(1.524&sup2; + 2.032&sup2;) = 2.540 mm</code> (in inches: <code class="mono">&radic;(0.0600&sup2; + 0.0800&sup2;) = 0.1000"</code>).</li>
            </ul>

            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(310px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-top: 0.85rem;">
              <div style="background: rgba(6, 182, 212, 0.06); border: 1px solid rgba(6, 182, 212, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.15rem;">
                <div class="mono" style="font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--cyan-400); font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">METRIC CALCULATION (G21)</div>
                <p style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.84rem; line-height: 1.7; margin: 0; color: #cbd5e1;">
                  Speed = 2540.0 mm/min<br />
                  Surface Distance = 2.540 mm<br />
                  &Delta;t = 2.540 / 2540.0 = 0.001 min (0.060s)<br />
                  <strong>F = 1 / 0.001 min = 1000.0</strong>
                </p>
              </div>

              <div style="background: rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.06); border: 1px solid rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.15rem;">
                <div class="mono" style="font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--amber-400); font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">IMPERIAL CALCULATION (G20)</div>
                <p style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.84rem; line-height: 1.7; margin: 0; color: #cbd5e1;">
                  Speed = 100.0 in/min (2540 &divide; 25.4)<br />
                  Surface Distance = 0.1000 in (2.540 &divide; 25.4)<br />
                  &Delta;t = 0.1000 / 100.0 = 0.001 min (0.060s)<br />
                  <strong>F = 1 / 0.001 min = 1000.0</strong>
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout danger" style="margin-top: 1.35rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">🛑 Why Dividing a G93 F Word by 25.4 Destroys Your Pipe</div>
              <p>
                Notice what happened: Both the speed (<code class="mono">2540 / 25.4 = 100</code>) and the distance (<code class="mono">2.54 / 25.4 = 0.100</code>) were divided by 25.4. <strong>The 25.4 unit factor cancels out completely!</strong>
              </p>
              <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                If a CAM post-processor mistakenly divided a <code class="mono">G93</code> <code class="mono">F</code> word by 25.4 when exporting Imperial G-code:
              </p>
              <div style="background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5); padding: 0.65rem 1rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); margin: 0.5rem 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.84rem; color: #f43f5e;">
                Wrong F = 1000.0 &divide; 25.4 = F39.37 &emsp;&rarr;&emsp; Move Time &Delta;t = 60 / 39.37 = 1.524 seconds!
              </div>
              <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                The move takes <strong>1.524 seconds instead of 0.060 seconds (25.4 times too slow).</strong> Your cutting speed drops from 100 IPM down to 3.94 IPM. The plasma arc stays stationary over the pipe wall, melts a massive blowout crater, ruins the nozzle shield, and destroys the workpiece.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- G94 vs G93 Hybrid Explanation -->
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: #fff; margin: 1.75rem 0 0.5rem;">The Hybrid Reality: G94 Probing vs. G93 Rotary Cutting</h3>
            <p>
              Inside a single 4-axis rotary G-code file, both <code class="mono">G94</code> and <code class="mono">G93</code> exist together, and different controllers handle surface touch-off differently:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Initial Height Sensing (IHS) &amp; Plunges Run in <code class="mono">G94</code>:</strong> Before the arc fires, surface touch-off probing (<code class="mono">G38.2</code> on grblHAL / bare LinuxCNC, <code class="mono">G31</code> on ArtSoft Mach3 / Mach4 / UCCNC) and vertical plunges to pierce/cut height move pure linear Z distance. They run in <code class="mono">G94</code> distance-per-minute mode. In <code class="mono">G21</code>, the probe moves at <code class="mono">F1000.0 mm/min</code>; in <code class="mono">G20</code>, the probe moves at <code class="mono">F39.4 IPM</code> (<code class="mono">1000 &divide; 25.4</code>).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Integrated vs. Explicit Touch-Off Loops (LinuxCNC QtPlasmaC &amp; MASSO G3):</strong>
                On <strong>2D Flat Sheet</strong> (no <code class="mono">#&lt;tube_cut&gt;</code>, zero Z/A words), <strong>LinuxCNC QtPlasmaC</strong> runs ohmic/float touch-off, pierce height, pierce delay, and cut-height plunge internally inside <code class="mono">M3 $0 S1</code> (ended with <code class="mono">M5 $0</code>), using <code class="mono">F#&lt;_hal[plasmac.cut-feed-rate]&gt;</code>. On <strong>Rotary &amp; 3D Ramps</strong> (<code class="mono">#&lt;tube_cut&gt;=1</code> before motion), QtPlasmaC yields Z control back to G-code: RotaryCAM probes explicitly with <code class="mono">G91/G38.2/G90/G1 Z#5063/G10 L20 P0</code>, executes <code class="mono">M3 $0 S1</code> + <code class="mono">G94 G1 Z</code> plunges, and uses rotary lookahead (<code class="mono">G94</code> on flat HSS faces switching to <code class="mono">G93</code> on <code class="mono">A</code> moves). Similarly, <strong>MASSO G3 + DTHC</strong> handles probing and pierce timing automatically inside <code class="mono">M3</code> via its <code class="mono">G200</code> plasma cycle.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Contour Cutting Runs in <code class="mono">G93</code>:</strong> As soon as the torch fires and lateral/rotary cutting begins, the program switches to <code class="mono">G93</code>. Every contour line carries an inverse-time <code class="mono">F</code> word that paces the move to the millisecond.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Side by Side G-code Comparison -->
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(310px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-top: 1rem;">
              <div class="doc-code-block">
                <div class="doc-code-bar">
                  <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot cyan"></span> G21 Metric 4-Axis Rotary (grblHAL 4-Axis Rotary)</span>
                  <span class="doc-code-lang">G-CODE</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code>G21 ; Millimeters
G90 ; Absolute positioning
M190 P-1 ; Explicit CAM Z (pierce_height = 0)
M65 P3 ; Clear torch inhibit
M64 P2 ; Lock THC (active-high disable)
G94 ; Start in G94 for touch-off probe
G00 X50.000 A0.000
G38.2 Z-25.0 F1000.0 ; Probe at 1000 mm/min (grblHAL)
G92 Z0
G00 Z3.800 ; Retract to pierce height
M03 S1 ; Torch fire
G04 P0.6 ; Pierce delay
G01 Z1.500 F1500.0 ; Plunge at 1500 mm/min
G93 ; SWITCH TO G93 INVERSE-TIME FOR ROTARY
G01 X51.524 A4.584 F1000.0 ; 0.060s move (2540 mm/min surface speed)
G01 X53.048 A9.167 F1000.0 ; 0.060s move (2540 mm/min surface speed)
M05 ; Torch off
G94 F1500.0 ; Restore G94 + F right after G93 (Core Issue #1027)
