        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 4
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-4">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 04</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">SimCNC &lt; 3.6 4D Trajectory Spoofing (<code class="mono">SimCNC Rotary</code>) vs. <code class="mono">SimCNC 3.6+</code> (<code class="mono">G93</code>)</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              CSLab SimCNC (running CSMIO/IP-M and IP-S controllers) provides hardware trajectory generation and sub-millisecond motion-synchronized digital pins (<code class="mono">M62/M63</code>). However, how SimCNC executes rotary motion depends on your software version:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>SimCNC &lt; 3.6 (e.g. Stable 3.5):</strong> Does not support native <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time mode. In standard <code class="mono">G94</code> mode, SimCNC's motion planner calculates trajectory displacement across all active axes as a <strong>4D Euclidean vector</strong>:
                <div style="background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5); padding: 0.85rem 1.15rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); margin: 0.75rem 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--emerald-400);">
                  L_4D = &radic;(&Delta;X&sup2; + &Delta;Y&sup2; + &Delta;Z&sup2; + &Delta;A&sup2;)
                </div>
                where <code class="mono">X, Y, Z</code> are in linear units (millimeters or inches) and <code class="mono">A</code> is in angular degrees (<code class="mono">&deg;</code>). RotaryCAM automatically calculates the spoofed <code class="mono">F</code> word for every line.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>SimCNC 3.6+ (Native <code class="mono">G93</code> Inverse-Time):</strong> CSLab added native <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time support in SimCNC 3.6+. Under the <code class="mono">SimCNC 3.6+</code> profile, RotaryCAM emits hardened <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time blocks (identical in <code class="mono">G20</code> and <code class="mono">G21</code>, formatted to <code class="mono">4</code> decimals with a <code class="mono">0.0001</code> floor) without 4D vector spoofing. Controller-safe rules emit <code class="mono">G69</code> plus metric <code class="mono">G64 P0.3</code>/<code class="mono">P0.4</code> blending even in <code class="mono">G20</code> programs (banning <code class="mono">G61</code>), coalesce <code class="mono">&lt; 0.05 mm</code> / <code class="mono">&lt; 0.02&deg;</code> micro-segments, apply <code class="mono">0.002 mm</code> (<code class="mono">0.0026 mm</code> in <code class="mono">G20</code>) axial feathering between pure-XYZ and pure-ABC moves, suppress sub-<code class="mono">1 ms</code> dwells (<code class="mono">G4 P&ge;1</code> ms), enforce the <code class="mono">#1999</code> parameter ceiling (with <code class="mono">#4061</code> THC allowlist and <code class="mono">G4 P1</code> sync), format <code class="mono">5</code>-decimal <code class="mono">G20</code> arcs (<code class="mono">I/J/K</code> within <code class="mono">0.005 mm</code>, split full circles, no <code class="mono">G90.1</code>/<code class="mono">G91.1</code>), cap comments at 1 <code class="mono">(...)</code> per line, and emit phase-preserving <code class="mono">G92 A</code> unwinds (no <code class="mono">G92.1</code>, explicit <code class="mono">G0</code>/<code class="mono">G1</code> reset after <code class="mono">G80</code>).
              </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: #fff; margin: 1.25rem 0 0.5rem;">How RotaryCAM "Spoofs" SimCNC's 4D Feedrate (SimCNC &lt; 3.6)</h3>
            <p>
              Because SimCNC does not know the physical tube radius in its trajectory planner, a standard linear <code class="mono">F</code> word would cause rotary cuts to crawl or race. RotaryCAM's kinematics engine solves this by calculating the true required move duration <code class="mono">&Delta;t</code> from physical surface speed, then reverse-engineering the exact spoofed <code class="mono">F</code> number that forces SimCNC's 4D vector equation to complete the move at the correct time:
            </p>
            <div style="background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5); padding: 0.75rem 1rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); margin: 0.5rem 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--amber-400);">
              F_spoofed = L_4D / &Delta;t = &radic;(&Delta;X&sup2; + &Delta;Y&sup2; + &Delta;Z&sup2; + &Delta;A&sup2;) / &Delta;t
            </div>

            <h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: #fff; margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;">What Happens in <code class="mono">G21</code> vs. <code class="mono">G20</code> Under SimCNC 4D?</h3>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Pure Rotary Chuck Spins (&Delta;X = &Delta;Y = &Delta;Z = 0):</strong>
                When only the chuck rotates, <code class="mono">L_4D = |&Delta;A|</code> degrees. In <code class="mono">G94</code> / SimCNC &lt; 3.6, the feedrate represents degrees per minute:
                <div style="background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5); padding: 0.5rem 0.85rem; border-radius: 4px; margin: 0.35rem 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--cyan-400);">
                  F_pure_A = (&Delta;A / &Delta;t) = (Surface Speed / (&pi; &times; OD)) &times; 360&deg;
                </div>
                Because angular rotational speed in degrees/minute (and chuck RPM) for a given surface speed is determined strictly by the tube OD, <strong>pure-A rotary moves keep the exact same F value in both G21 and G20!</strong>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Combined X+A Diagonal Moves (Fishmouths &amp; Miters):</strong>
                In <code class="mono">G20</code>, <code class="mono">&Delta;X</code> is in inches (<code class="mono">&Delta;X_mm &divide; 25.4</code>), while <code class="mono">&Delta;A</code> remains in degrees. The 4D hypotenuse changes ratio between inches and degrees, and RotaryCAM scales <code class="mono">F_spoofed</code> on every line so the physical surface cutting speed across the tube wall remains completely constant at your dialed-in cut-chart IPM.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <div class="doc-code-block" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-code-bar">
                <span class="doc-code-title"><span class="dot emerald"></span> SimCNC 4D Rotary G-Code Example (rot-test-saddle1.step)</span>
                <span class="doc-code-lang">G-CODE</span>
              </div>
              <pre><code>(--- SimCNC 4D Feedrate Scaling: 27mm OD Pipe Saddle ---)
G21 ; Metric mode (X in mm, A in degrees)
G90 ; Absolute positioning
G94 ; Standard G94 with per-line 4D trajectory spoofing (SimCNC < 3.6)
G00 Z25.000 ; Safe Z clearance
G00 X-104.043 Y0.000
G01 A51.702 F1500.0 ; Chuck index (pure A in deg/min)
M03 S1 ; Ohmic touch-off, pierce height & arc fire
M63 P0 ; Lock THC during lead-in
; 4D interpolated feedrates maintain constant 1000 mm/min surface cut speed:
G01 X-103.794 A52.214 F2059.043 ; Spoofed F blends mm and deg
G01 X-103.410 A52.952 F1974.067
M62 P0 ; Unlock THC for main contour cut
G01 X-99.343 A58.857 F1513.375
G01 X-99.014 A62.132 F3780.151 ; Chuck accelerates smoothly around saddle
G01 X-94.771 A95.767 F3650.210 ; Saddle crotch
M63 P1 ; Motion-sync digital output cuts arc 1.5mm early while torch moves (zero divot)
G01 X-91.916 A117.747 F3820.440 ; Final segment severs kerf cleanly on residual plasma
M05 ; Motion complete: full torch shutdown macro
G00 Z25.000 ; Retract</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout warning" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">⚠️ The SimCNC M05 Deceleration Trap (Why M63 Px is Required for Zero-Divot Cuts)</div>
              <p>
                In SimCNC, calling standard <code class="mono">M05</code> triggers a Python macro (<code class="mono">m5.py</code>). SimCNC's trajectory planner is <strong>not motion-synchronized with standard M-codes</strong>: it forces the motion planner to decelerate axes to a complete standstill before executing <code class="mono">m5.py</code>.
              </p>
              <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
                Stopping the torch while the plasma arc is burning gouges an ugly blowout divot right into the cut edge! To achieve a true zero-divot cut, RotaryCAM triggers a motion-synchronized hardware pin (<code class="mono">M63 Px</code>) roughly 1.5–2.0 mm before the end of the toolpath. The plasma arc extinguishes instantly while the torch continues moving at full programmed cutting speed, allowing residual plasma gas to sever the kerf. Standard <code class="mono">M05</code> is then called only after motion has come to a stop.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

