            <div class="doc-callout tip" style="margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">💡 Controller Probing Notes: MASSO G3, LinuxCNC QtPlasmaC, WinCNC &amp; Generic RS-274</div>
              <p>
                <strong>MASSO G3 + DTHC:</strong> MASSO uses <code class="mono">T112 M6</code> followed by <code class="mono">G200 F... P... D... C... V...</code>, where <code class="mono">M3</code> automatically executes torch touch-off probing, pierce delay, and plunge to cut height without requiring manual <code class="mono">G38.2 Z</code> + <code class="mono">G92 Z0</code> blocks in standard cutting. (Manual probing is only used in legacy external-THC or custom dynamic-4D-Z setups). Note that the plunge/cut feedrate parameter <code class="mono">F</code> inside <code class="mono">G200</code> still divides by 25.4 when running in Imperial <code class="mono">G20</code>.
              </p>
              <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>LinuxCNC 2.9+ QtPlasmaC:</strong> On <strong>2D Flat Sheet</strong> (zero Z/A words, no <code class="mono">#&lt;tube_cut&gt;</code>), IHS, pierce delay, and cut plunge execute internally via <code class="mono">M3 $0 S1</code> (and shut off with <code class="mono">M5 $0</code>), so never emit <code class="mono">G38.2</code> or Z words there. On <strong>Rotary &amp; 3D Ramps</strong> (<code class="mono">#&lt;tube_cut&gt;=1</code> before motion), QtPlasmaC hands Z control to G-code: RotaryCAM runs explicit <code class="mono">G91/G38.2/G90/G1 Z#5063/G10 L20 P0</code> probing and <code class="mono">M3 $0 S1</code> + <code class="mono">G94 G1 Z</code> plunges. (<code class="mono">#&lt;keep-z-motion&gt;=1</code> is reserved strictly for non-<code class="mono">M3</code> scribes and markers.)
              </p>
              <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>ShopSabre WinCNC (<code class="mono">v3.1.06d+</code> via <code class="mono">Help &rarr; About</code>):</strong> WinCNC reads <code class="mono">G21</code> as <em>centimeters</em>, so RotaryCAM strictly emits <code class="mono">G22</code> for metric millimeters and <code class="mono">G20</code> for inches (never <code class="mono">G21</code>). Match your SideKick rotary axis letter (<code class="mono">A</code>, <code class="mono">W</code>, or <code class="mono">B</code>) to <code class="mono">WINCNC.INI</code>. Programs emit <code class="mono">M65</code> init, optional <code class="mono">G9 S10</code>, <code class="mono">G94</code> before rapids and <code class="mono">M62</code>, <code class="mono">L28.1</code> 0-360 rotary wraps, Pack-only <code class="mono">L92</code> before <code class="mono">M66</code> (preserving single-part <code class="mono">G92</code> restore), <code class="mono">G4 X&lt;sec&gt;</code> dwells, <code class="mono">L37.2 D1/D0</code> continuous THC (<code class="mono">D1</code> = Disabled, <code class="mono">D0</code> = Enabled) vs. legacy <code class="mono">L37/L36</code>, decoupled <code class="mono">L38.1</code> (<code class="mono">THC=... M1</code> vs. <code class="mono">M0</code>), <code class="mono">M61</code> vs. <code class="mono">L38</code> cycles, and <code class="mono">G54</code>-baked pneumatic scribe XY offsets.
              </p>
              <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>Portable RS-274 (<code class="mono">generic_g94</code>):</strong> Assumes a pre-zeroed top-surface <code class="mono">Z0</code> datum by default and uses a strict core subset (<code class="mono">G00/G01/G02/G03/G04/G17/G20-G21/G40/G90/G92/G94</code>, <code class="mono">M03/M05/M30</code>) with uppercase <code class="mono">(COMMENT)</code> blocks (&le; 80 chars) and zero <code class="mono">;</code>, <code class="mono">%</code>, <code class="mono">G49</code>, <code class="mono">G92.1</code>, or <code class="mono">G10 L20</code>. THC defaults to <code class="mono">None</code> (printing a header THC-lock warning on 3D ramps and 4D Z motion) with optional <code class="mono">M62/M63</code> or custom M-codes, configurable <code class="mono">G04</code> dwells (<code class="mono">P0.500</code>, <code class="mono">P500</code>, <code class="mono">X0.500</code>, or off; clamped &ge; 10 ms with forced decimal), <code class="mono">&lt; 0.005 mm</code> linear snap, split 3D ramp <code class="mono">M03</code> ignition, swing-envelope Safe Z before <code class="mono">A</code> rapids, and Dry-Run mode. Not compatible with Burny, Dynatorch, or FlashCut Stingray.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- 5 Golden Shop Rules -->
            <div class="doc-callout info">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">📋 5 Shop Rules to Remember at the Machine Console</div>
              <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
                <li>
                  <strong>Rule 1 (Flat Sheet = Distance):</strong> On 2D plate and 3-axis Y-wrapped round pipe, feedrates are <code class="mono">G94</code> distance-per-minute. When flipping from metric to inches, <code class="mono">F</code> divides by 25.4 (<code class="mono">2540 mm/min &rarr; 100 IPM</code>).
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Rule 2 (4-Axis Rotary = Inverse Time):</strong> On 4-axis rotary tubes, cutting feedrates are <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time (<code class="mono">1 / &Delta;t</code>). Because both cutting speed and move distance divide by 25.4, the unit factor cancels out: <code class="mono">F1000.0</code> is identical in Metric and Imperial. Never divide a <code class="mono">G93</code> <code class="mono">F</code> word by 25.4.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Rule 3 (Probes &amp; Plunges Scale):</strong> Initial touch-off sensing (<code class="mono">G38.2</code> on grblHAL, <code class="mono">G31</code> on Mach3/Mach4/UCCNC, <code class="mono">G200</code> on MASSO) and vertical Z plunges run in <code class="mono">G94</code> linear mode before the arc strikes. Their feedrates <em>do</em> divide by 25.4 in Imperial (<code class="mono">1000 mm/min &rarr; 39.4 IPM</code>). On 2D Flat Sheet in LinuxCNC QtPlasmaC, probing, pierce height, and cut plunge run internally inside <code class="mono">M3 $0 S1</code>; on Rotary &amp; 3D Ramps (<code class="mono">#&lt;tube_cut&gt;=1</code>), RotaryCAM emits explicit <code class="mono">G38.2</code> probing and <code class="mono">G94 G1 Z</code> plunges.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Rule 4 (Pure A Rotations: G93 is ALWAYS Inverse Time, NEVER deg/min):</strong> When the chuck rotates without lateral motion (<code class="mono">&Delta;X = &Delta;Y = &Delta;Z = 0</code>), in <code class="mono">G93</code> mode <code class="mono">F</code> is still strictly inverse time (<code class="mono">1 / &Delta;t</code> in <code class="mono">min&sup1;</code>), NEVER degrees per minute! Only <code class="mono">G94</code> (such as <code class="mono">SimCNC &lt; 3.6</code>) expresses pure-A spins in <code class="mono">deg/min</code>. For example, at <code class="mono">5729.6&deg;/min</code> (<code class="mono">2540 mm/min / 100 IPM</code> surface speed on 2" / 50.8 mm OD pipe), a 90&deg; pure-A spin runs at <code class="mono">F5729.6</code> (<code class="mono">deg/min</code>) in <code class="mono">G94</code> / <code class="mono">SimCNC &lt; 3.6</code>, whereas in <code class="mono">G93</code> it runs at <code class="mono">F63.66</code> (<code class="mono">&Delta;t = 0.01571 min = 0.942 s</code>, <code class="mono">1 / &Delta;t = 63.66 min&sup1;</code>). Both values remain 100% identical between <code class="mono">G20</code> and <code class="mono">G21</code>, but only <code class="mono">G94</code> is <code class="mono">deg/min</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Rule 5 (Keep Override at 100% on Rotary Cuts):</strong> In <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time mode, Feedrate Override (FRO) scales every block's duration uniformly (e.g., dialing FRO down to 50% cleanly doubles every block's cut time). However, you should leave FRO at 100% on rotary plasma cuts for two critical physical reasons: (1) Slowing down below target surface speed widens the kerf and raises arc voltage, causing voltage-based Torch Height Control (THC) to dive the torch tip directly into the pipe wall; (2) Pushing FRO above 100% can clip against your rotary A-axis maximum velocity limit on tight saddle crotches or square tube corner sweeps, bottlenecking motion and creating torch gouges.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
